evidence_facts (attribute by name in prose; no extra links):
- Vicks VapoRub: 2011 JABFM case series, 18 adults, daily for 48 weeks: 15 (83%) improved, 5 (27.8%) full mycological + clinical cure, 10 (55.6%) partial, 3 no change. No placebo group.
- 100% tea tree oil: 1994 RCT (J Fam Pract), twice daily for 6 months: ≈18% mycological cure, ≈56% improved, comparable to clotrimazole. Mayo Clinic: effectiveness not shown; lower concentrations showed no benefit.
- Vinegar: no controlled trial on toenail fungus (WebMD, Healthline). ACV can cause chemical burns (Cleveland Clinic).
- Baking soda: lab nail samples, growth stopped in 79% (WebMD); prevents more than kills; can over-dry feet (Cleveland Clinic).
- Hydrogen peroxide: generally considered ineffective in practice (Global Nail Fungus Organization, via Healthline). Listerine: never studied for toenail fungus (WebMD). Garlic: lab only (GoodRx); raw garlic can chemically burn (SingleCare).
- Penetration: topicals struggle to reach the nail unit; home filing/debridement helps (Univ. of Utah Health dermatologist).
- Timeline: toenail takes up to 12-18 months to replace itself (ScienceInsights, SingleCare); WebMD: 6+ months before nails look better.
- Fit: home care only for mild cases (under half of 1-2 nails, no pain, no thickening) (ScienceInsights). Skip DIY with diabetes, PAD, weak immunity, neuropathy (Cleveland Clinic).
- Cost hook (one line max): Harvard Health cites Vicks ≈$24/year versus several thousand dollars for a year of Jublia. Do not compare prescriptions (other post).

